## Claim
Text: "Speaker Robin Vos lost a Republican-held seat in the 2018 election for the first time."
Claim author: Robyn Vining
Claim date: October 25, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### What political office did Robin Vos hold in the state of Wisconsin during the November 2018 election?
Answer: During the November 2018 election, Robin Vos served as a representative in the Wisconsin State Assembly, having been "representing western Racine County in the Wisconsin State Assembly since 2005," and held the leadership role of "Speaker of the Assembly."

Source URL: https://thompsoncenter.wisc.edu/staff/vos-robin/

### Did Robin Vos lose his own seat in the Wisconsin State Assembly in the 2018 election?
Answer: No, Robin Vos did not lose his own seat in the 2018 election. According to the source, "Speaker Robin Vos has been representing western Racine County in the Wisconsin State Assembly since 2005," indicating he has held his seat continuously since that time.

Source URL: https://thompsoncenter.wisc.edu/staff/vos-robin/

### Did the Republican Party lose any seats in the Wisconsin State Assembly to another political party in the 2018 general election?
Answer: Yes, the Republican Party lost one seat in the Wisconsin State Assembly to the Democratic Party during the 2018 general election. According to the search result, the Republican Party won 63 seats, representing "a loss of one seat," while the Democratic Party gained "just one seat from the Republicans, District 14."

Source URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2018_Wisconsin_State_Assembly_election

### Which specific Wisconsin State Assembly districts transitioned from Republican control to Democratic control as a result of the 2018 election?
Answer: Based on the 2018 Wisconsin State Assembly election results, the Democratic Party gained "just one seat from the Republicans, District 14." Therefore, District 14 was the only specific district that transitioned from Republican to Democratic control as a result of the election.

Source URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2018_Wisconsin_State_Assembly_election

### In what year did Robin Vos assume the role of Speaker of the Wisconsin State Assembly?
Answer: According to the provided source, Robin Vos assumed the role of Speaker of the Wisconsin State Assembly in 2013, noting that "Vos, who has been speaker since 2013, is used to being at the center of Wisconsin's biggest political battles."

Source URL: https://www.cbs58.com/news/vos-emerges-as-wisconsin-republican-leader-as-walker-gets-ready-to-step-down

### Did the Republican Party lose any seats in the Wisconsin State Assembly during the 2016 Wisconsin State Assembly elections under Robin Vos's speakership?
Answer: No, the Republican Party did not lose any seats in the Wisconsin State Assembly during the 2016 elections. Instead, "Republicans increased their majority in the Wisconsin State Assembly from 63-36 to 64-35."

Source URL: https://ballotpedia.org/Wisconsin_State_Assembly_elections,_2018

### What was the total number of Republican-held seats in the Wisconsin State Assembly before and after the 2018 election?
Answer: Before the 2018 election, the Republican Party held 64 seats in the Wisconsin State Assembly, which decreased to 63 seats after the election. According to the search result, the Republicans maintained their majority by winning "63 seats, a loss of one seat" to the Democrats, who gained "just one seat from the Republicans, District 14."

Source URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2018_Wisconsin_State_Assembly_election

### Did Robyn Vining win a Wisconsin State Assembly seat in the 2018 election that was previously held by a Republican?
Answer: Yes, Robyn Vining won a Wisconsin State Assembly seat in the 2018 election that was previously held by a Republican. According to the source, Wisconsin Assembly District 14 "was occupied by Republican Dale Kooyenga for eight years until Democrat Robyn Vining defeated three candidates in 2018 to take the seat."

Source URL: https://www.jsonline.com/story/communities/west/news/brookfield/2020/07/22/primary-wisconsin-state-assembly-district-14-meet-candidates/5479961002/

## Final Judgement
### Step-by-step Thoughts

1. **Analyze the Claim**: The claim states that "Speaker Robin Vos lost a Republican-held seat in the 2018 election for the first time." This refers to Robin Vos in his capacity as the Speaker of the Wisconsin State Assembly (the leader of the Republican majority), under whose leadership the party lost a seat.
2. **Analyze the Evidence**:
   - Robin Vos has been in the Wisconsin State Assembly since 2005 and became Speaker in 2013.
   - In the 2018 election, Vos did not lose his own seat.
   - However, the Republican Party lost exactly one seat in the Assembly during the 2018 election (District 14) to the Democratic Party.
   - This seat (District 14) was won by the claim's author, Robyn Vining, and was previously held by Republican Dale Kooyenga.
   - In the previous election cycle under Vos's speakership (2016), the Republicans did not lose any seats; instead, they increased their majority.
   - Therefore, the 2018 election was indeed the first time under Speaker Vos's leadership that the Republicans lost a seat in the Assembly.
3. **Determine if more information is needed**: The retrieved evidence is clear, consistent, and directly addresses the claim. No further information is required.
4. **Formulate the Conclusion**: The claim is supported because, under Speaker Vos's leadership, the Republican Party lost an Assembly seat for the first time during the 2018 election when District 14 transitioned to Democratic control.

### Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence shows that Robin Vos has served in the Wisconsin State Assembly since 2005 and assumed the role of Speaker in 2013. While Vos did not lose his own legislative seat in the 2018 election, the Republican caucus under his leadership lost one seat (District 14) to Democratic candidate Robyn Vining. In the preceding 2016 election under Vos's speakership, the Republicans did not lose any seats and instead increased their majority. Thus, the 2018 election marked the first time under Speaker Vos's tenure that the Republicans lost a seat in the Assembly. Because the evidence is clear and complete, no further investigation is required.

### Conclusion
Based on the recorded evidence, the claim is `supported`. Although Speaker Robin Vos retained his own seat in the Wisconsin State Assembly, the Republican Party lost a seat under his leadership for the first time during the 2018 election. This occurred when District 14, previously held by a Republican, was won by Democrat Robyn Vining. Since the Republicans had previously expanded their majority in the 2016 election, the 2018 loss was indeed the first seat loss under Vos's speakership.

### Verdict: SUPPORTED

### Justification
The claim is supported because, while Speaker Robin Vos retained his own seat in the Wisconsin State Assembly according to the [Thompson Center](https://thompsoncenter.wisc.edu/staff/vos-robin/), the Republican Party lost a seat under his leadership for the first time during the 2018 election. [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2018_Wisconsin_State_Assembly_election) records show that the Republicans lost District 14, which transitioned to Democratic control when Robyn Vining won the seat previously held by Republican Dale Kooyenga, as reported by the [Milwaukee Journal Sentinel](https://www.jsonline.com/story/communities/west/news/brookfield/2020/07/22/primary-wisconsin-state-assembly-district-14-meet-candidates/5479961002/). Since Vos became Speaker in 2013, as noted by [CBS 58](https://www.cbs58.com/news/vos-emerges-as-wisconsin-republican-leader-as-walker-gets-ready-to-step-down), and the Republicans previously expanded their majority in the 2016 election according to [Ballotpedia](https://ballotpedia.org/Wisconsin_State_Assembly_elections,_2018), the 2018 loss was indeed the first seat loss under his speakership.